        ans: 0,
        exp: "Rule: At common law, burglary requires: (1) breaking, (2) entering, (3) the dwelling house, (4) of another, (5) in the nighttime, (6) with the intent to commit a felony therein (such as robbery). Under common law breaking rules, pushing open a door that is already partially ajar (even by a few inches) constitutes a legal **breaking** because the actor applies physical force to create an opening large enough to enter. Defendant broke and entered an occupied dwelling house at 11:00 PM (nighttime). Crucially, the crime of burglary is fully complete at the precise instant the actor enters with the requisite felonious intent; whether the intended felony is completed, or proves factually impossible because there are no valuables present, is completely irrelevant. Defendant entered intending to commit robbery (a felony), completing common law burglary (Option A). Options B and D are incorrect because completed burglary ranks above attempt or trespass. Option C is incorrect because Defendant did not commit completed robbery (no property was taken)."
